 resolution  to avoid additional harm to the american  people the country's going through  enough  it doesn't need the drama of washington  d.c  to add to it lawmakers return to the  nation's capital after the labor day  holiday  reporting in washington i'm morgan  wright  the last u.s federal government shutdown  started on december  22nd 2018 and lasted 35 days  it was the longest u.s government  shutdown in history  a small business group is suing the  trump administration for the names of  all businesses that got paycheck  protection program loans  and the exact amounts the american small  business league says so far  americans have little information or  transparency  about how the money was spent lloyd  chapman is the organization's president  publicly rated companies on the new york  stock exchange  and billionaires getting this money  congress is starting to look into who  received those loans as well  john langler talked with our washington  dc bureau correspondent  alexandra limon about it alex let's talk  about some of these problems what are  they talking about on capitol hill to  try and  deal with this yeah there's actually a  brand new congressional investigation  looking into ppp loans that was released  just this week  and what those lawmakers found was about  1 billion dollars  in potentially fraudulent ppp loans the  reason is that these democratic house  members say  that businesses some businesses obtained  more than one ppp  loan and that is actually a violation of  the program's rules  and they say those loans those duplicate  loans totaled about a billion  dollars so that is one big problem right  there but this congressional  investigation actually uncovered  a long list of other potential problems  with the ppp loans for example  they say there were hundreds of  applications for ppp loans that didn't  have any names  or addresses on the applications and  they were still  approved so lawmakers say in some of  these cases we don't even really know  who that money went to now treasury  secretary steven mnuchin was grilled  about this  issue in a hearing this week on capitol  hill and what he said was that any ppp  loan that is worth more than  two million dollars will be audited and  any  loan that was smaller than that can  possibly be audited and democrats were  pretty unhappy with that answer  because they're concerned that that  means that a majority of these ppp loans  will never get looked at  considering the speed behind getting the  ppp  off the ground the amount of money  involved are lawmakers  surprised that there's been this level  of fraud there's been this level of sort  of  lost money essentially with such an  enormous program  well i don't know if they're truly  surprised because you're right these  loans and this program was approved  so quickly but these lawmakers certainly  do act like they're surprised they say  that this shouldn't be happening  and they certainly seem outraged but it  is a good question you know whether  anyone can really be surprised  because you know those loans were  approved so quickly and in fact now  congress is trying to approve a third  round of possible ppp loans  that was alexandra limon reporting from  washington d.c
 

